###reported bugs
1. Sometimes (especially when a large number of CPU processors are sued), segmentation fault appears when the solver runs the subroutine src/specfem3D/locate_source_cubed.f90. Wenbo found that replacing NPROC with a specified number, which is the number of CPU processors, can solve this issue. Still do not know why.

##################################################################################################
2. The seismograms generated by SPECFEM are delayed by one SEM time step relative to the injected wavefield. This time lag arises 
because the imposed wavefield corresponds to the solution at t+Δt, as illustrated below.
#! explicit Newmark time scheme with acoustic & elastic domains:
#! (see e.g. Hughes, 1987; Chaljub et al., 2003)
#!
#! chi(t+delta_t) = chi(t) + delta_t chi_dot(t) + 1/2 delta_t**2 chi_dot_dot(t)
#! chi_dot(t+delta_t) = chi_dot(t) + 1/2 delta_t chi_dot_dot(t) + 1/2 delta_t chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
#! chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t) = 1/M_acoustic( -K_acoustic chi(t+delta) + B_acoustic u(t+delta_t) + f(t+delta_t) )
#!
#! u(t+delta_t) = u(t) + delta_t  v(t) + 1/2  delta_t**2 a(t)
#! v(t+delta_t) = v(t) + 1/2 delta_t a(t) + 1/2 delta_t a(t+delta_t)
#! a(t+delta_t) = 1/M_elastic ( -K_elastic u(t+delta) + B_elastic chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t) + f( t+delta_t) )
#!
#! where
#!   chi, chi_dot, chi_dot_dot are acoustic (fluid) potentials ( dotted with respect to time)
#!   u, v, a are displacement,velocity & acceleration
#!   M is mass matrix, K stiffness matrix and B boundary term for acoustic/elastic domains
#!   f denotes a source term (acoustic/elastic)
#!
#! note that this stage calculates the predictor terms
#!
#!   for
#!   potential chi_dot(t+delta) requires + 1/2 delta_t chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
#!                                   at a later stage (corrector) once where chi_dot_dot(t+delta) is calculated
#!   and similar,
#!   velocity v(t+delta_t) requires  + 1/2 delta_t a(t+delta_t)
#!                                   at a later stage once where a(t+delta) is calculated
#! also:
#!   boundary term B_elastic requires chi_dot_dot(t+delta)
#!                                   thus chi_dot_dot has to be updated first before the elastic boundary term is considered
#

The subroutine iterate_time.F90 works on the below tasks one by one,
a. call update_displacement_scheme:
   u(t+delta_t) = u(t) + delta_t  v(t)  + 1/2  delta_t**2 a(t)
   v(t+delta_t) = v(t) + 1/2 delta_t a(t)
   chi(t+delta_t) = chi(t) + delta_t chi_dot(t) + 1/2 delta_t**2 chi_dot_dot(t)
   chi_dot(t+delta_t) = chi_dot(t) + 1/2 delta_t chi_dot_dot(t) 
b. call compute_forces_acoustic_calling to compute chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t):
      ###potential_dot_dot_acoustic corresponds to chi_dot_dot in above step1. Similarly, potential_dot->chi_dot and potential->chi 
      call compute_forces_acoustic_NGLL5_fast:
          potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) = potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) + temp4(i,j,k) #-K_acoustic chi(t+delta)


      If boundary elemens,
          If couping scheme, call compute_imposed_forces_acoustic:
                                potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) = potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) &
                                   +jacobianw*displ_n &
                                   -(potential_dot_acoustic(iglob)-chi_dot0) * jacobianw / cpl / rhol  #imposing pressure and absorbing scattering wave
                        or call compute_stacey_acoustic for non-injection scheme:
          else, call compute_stacey_acoustic
                                potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) = potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) -
                                    potential_dot_acoustic(iglob) * jacobianw / cpl / rhol #absorbing full wave


      call compute_coupling_acoustic_el to deal with elastic-acoustic coupling:
          potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) = potential_dot_dot_acoustic(iglob) + jacobianw*displ_n #B_acoustic u(t+delta_t)


      potential_dot_dot_acoustic(:) = potential_dot_dot_acoustic(:) * rmass_acoustic(:) #1/M_acoustic


      ##! chi_dot(t+delta_t) = chi_dot(t+delta_t)  + 1/2 delta_t chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
      potential_dot_acoustic(:) = potential_dot_acoustic(:) + deltatover2*potential_dot_dot_acoustic(:)
          
c. call compute_forces_viscoelastic_calling_routine to compute a(t+delta_t):
      call compute_forces_viscoelastic
          ##################the strain on the Green's function grids are also computed here. Thus, they are epsilon(t+delta).#####

          accel(1,iglob) = accel(1,iglob) - (fac1 * newtempx1(i,j,k) + fac2 * newtempx2(i,j,k) + fac3 * newtempx3(i,j,k)) #-K_elastic u(t+delta)
          accel(2,iglob) = accel(2,iglob) - (fac1 * newtempy1(i,j,k) + fac2 * newtempy2(i,j,k) + fac3 * newtempy3(i,j,k)) #-K_elastic u(t+delta)
          accel(3,iglob) = accel(3,iglob) - (fac1 * newtempz1(i,j,k) + fac2 * newtempz2(i,j,k) + fac3 * newtempz3(i,j,k)) #-K_elastic u(t+delta)
 
      If boundary elemens,,
          If couping scheme, call compute_imposed_forces_elastic

                                        tx = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn_scattering*nx + &
			                rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vx_scattering-vn_scattering*nx)
 				        ty = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn_scattering*ny + &
			                rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vy_scattering-vn_scattering*ny)
				        tz = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn_scattering*nz + &
			                rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vz_scattering-vn_scattering*nz)
                                        accel(1,iglob) = accel(1,iglob) + (traction_imposed(1)-tx)*jacobianw #imposing traction and aborbing scattering waves
                                        accel(2,iglob) = accel(2,iglob) + (traction_imposed(2)-ty)*jacobianw #imposing traction and aborbing scattering waves
                                        accel(3,iglob) = accel(3,iglob) + (traction_imposed(3)-tz)*jacobianw #imposing traction and aborbing scattering waves
          else, call compute_stacey_viscoelastic
				        tx = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn*nx + rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vx-vn*nx)
				        ty = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn*ny + rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vy-vn*ny)
				        tz = rho_vp(i,j,k,ispec)*vn*nz + rho_vs(i,j,k,ispec)*(vz-vn*nz)
				        accel(1,iglob) = accel(1,iglob) - tx*jacobianw #absorbing full wave
				        accel(2,iglob) = accel(2,iglob) - ty*jacobianw #absorbing full wave
				        accel(3,iglob) = accel(3,iglob) - tz*jacobianw #absorbing full wave
     call compute_coupling_viscoelastic_ac:
                accel(1,iglob) = accel(1,iglob) + jacobianw*nx*pressure #+B_elastic chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
	        accel(2,iglob) = accel(2,iglob) + jacobianw*ny*pressure #+B_elastic chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
	        accel(3,iglob) = accel(3,iglob) + jacobianw*nz*pressure #+B_elastic chi_dot_dot(t+delta_t)
     accel(1,:) = accel(1,:)*rmassx(:) #1/M_elastic
     accel(2,:) = accel(2,:)*rmassy(:)
     accel(3,:) = accel(3,:)*rmassz(:)

    ##! v(t+delta_t) = v(t+delta_t)) + 1/2 delta_t a(t+delta_t)
    veloc(:,:) = veloc(:,:) + deltatover2*accel(:,:)

d. call SEMtoTele()
      #both traction and displacement are saved at the time of t+delta_t.
      call compute_traction_disp_elastic()
          traction_bound(it_bound_thispack,1,ipoint)=normal_vector(1)*sigma_xxtpl+ &
               normal_vector(2)*sigma_xytpl+normal_vector(3)*sigma_xztpl
          traction_bound(it_bound_thispack,2,ipoint)=normal_vector(1)*sigma_xytpl+ &
               normal_vector(2)*sigma_yytpl+normal_vector(3)*sigma_yztpl
          traction_bound(it_bound_thispack,3,ipoint)=normal_vector(1)*sigma_xztpl+ &
               normal_vector(2)*sigma_yztpl+normal_vector(3)*sigma_zztpl

          disp_bound(it_bound_thispack,:,ipoint)=displ(:,ibool(i,j,k,ispec))
############################################################################################################################
3. Using the Low-Dissipation and Low-Dispersion Runge–Kutta (LDDRK) time-integration scheme can effectively reduce temporal dispersion. However, achieving 
a level of accuracy comparable to that obtained with the Newmark scheme generally requires a similar, or even slightly higher, computational cost than using 
the Newmark scheme with a reduced time step. Moreover, LDDRK may produce relatively large errors in weak back-scattered and reflected waves, and these 
errors cannot be eliminated by mesh refinement. This limitation is likely caused by a mismatch between the computed wavefield and its temporal 
representation, as the wavefield is evaluated at an intermediate time, Δt×(istep−1+fraction), rather than at the discrete time levels used in the hybrid coupling.

4. Fluid–solid interface topography (ICB and CMB):
The code may misclassify elements near fluid–solid interfaces (e.g., the ICB and CMB) when the interface curvature is large. In such cases, 
determining the domain (fluid or solid) based solely on the element centroid becomes unreliable because the geometric error due to curvature 
may exceed half the element size. Possible remedies include smoothing the interface topography or refining the mesh (i.e., reducing the element size).
